# Shatter — Debug and automation API

Shatter ships a small debugging and automation surface so the game can be driven and
inspected from code, without touching the keyboard or waiting on real time. It is
what you use to iterate on the flight and gravity, reproduce a specific encounter,
write automated checks of the mechanics, and capture clean screenshots of an exact
game state. This file defines that surface. Implement all of it, on the same footing
as the game itself.

Nothing here changes how a person plays. The debug API is inert during normal play,
doing nothing until something calls it, and the debug overlay is off until toggled.

## A deterministic core

The whole surface rests on the simulation being deterministic and steppable, which
the loop in `specs/simulation.md` already requires: a fixed timestep of 120 Hz,
integrated in whole steps, decoupled from rendering. Two properties make it
driveable from code:

- Render-free core. Game state advances by stepping the simulation and must not
  depend on a canvas, on `requestAnimationFrame`, or on wall-clock time to make
  progress. Rendering reads the state, never the other way around.
- Seeded randomness. Any randomness the game uses (a rock's spawn position and drift,
  the saucer's entry and aim) runs off a seedable generator, so reseeding and
  replaying the same calls reproduces the same result exactly.

Given the same seed and the same sequence of API calls and steps, the game reaches
the same state every time.

## The manual clock

During normal play the game advances itself: an animation-frame loop reads the
elapsed wall-clock time each frame and steps the simulation by it. The debug API can
take that clock over, so a scripted scenario advances by exact amounts rather than by
however much real time happened to pass.

The unit that clock is driven in is the simulation tick — one fixed step of the
120 Hz timestep, so exactly 1/120 of a second of game time. Ticks rather than seconds
are the honest unit for a fixed-timestep sim: a seconds argument has to be turned into
a whole number of steps somewhere, and rounding it silently moves the simulation a
different distance than the caller asked for. Asking in ticks means what is asked for
is what runs.

The game holds an `autoStep` flag, `true` by default (normal human play). Each
animation frame the game still renders, but it only advances the simulation when
`autoStep` is `true`.

- `reset()` and `step()` both set `autoStep` to `false`, beginning a
  driver-clocked session. While it is `false`, `step(ticks)` is the only thing that
  advances the simulation, so no stray wall-clock frame can slip in and a stepped
  scenario is exact and reproducible.
- `setAutoStep(true)` hands the clock back to the game so it advances itself in real
  time again (useful for watching a scenario play out or recording a motion clip);
  `setAutoStep(false)` returns to manual stepping. A `reset` re-arms manual stepping.
- Injecting input (`keyDown`/`keyUp`/`press`) and the other control operations do not
  change `autoStep`.

Rendering runs every frame regardless of the flag, so the game always draws its
current state whether the clock is manual or live.

## The `window.__shatter` object

Expose the API as a single object on the global `window.__shatter`, installed once
the game is running. It carries a `version` number (use `1`) and the operations
below. Values are plain numbers, strings, and booleans so a caller can read them
directly; coordinates and velocities are in the logical-pixel space of
`specs/overview.md`, and angles are in radians.

### Core operations

- `reset(options)` returns the game to its initial title state and re-arms manual
  stepping. `options` is optional, and `options.seed` (a number) seeds all of the
  game's randomness so a scenario replays identically.
- `step(ticks)` advances the simulation by exactly `ticks` fixed steps immediately,
  running the fixed-timestep update internally rather than waiting for real frames,
  and sets `autoStep` to `false`. The unit is whole simulation ticks, not seconds:
  the timestep is 120 Hz, so one tick is 1/120 of a second, `step(1)` runs a single
  physics step and `step(120)` advances one second of game time. Nothing is rounded
  or approximated — the number of steps asked for is the number of steps run.
  `ticks` must be a non-negative integer; `step(0)` is legal and does nothing, while
  a fractional or negative value is invalid and the call fails loudly rather than
  guessing what was meant. Stepping advances the live game (the ship, bullets,
  rocks, saucer, and waves) and has no effect on a menu screen.
- `setAutoStep(enabled)` sets the manual clock described above: `false` (the default
  after `reset` or `step`) means `step` is the sole way the simulation advances;
  `true` lets the game advance itself in real time again.
- `snapshot()` returns a plain, JSON-serializable object describing the current game
  state (see [Snapshot shape](#snapshot-shape)). It is a pure read and never changes
  anything.

### Control operations

These set up a specific situation. Each one routes through the same systems normal
play uses, arranging the world rather than faking outcomes: a control operation
places bodies or sets a value, then `step()` runs the real simulation forward and
`snapshot()` reads what happened.

- `startGame()` starts a new game, exactly as choosing `PLAY` from the title would.
  It enters the in-game state and spawns the first wave.
- `setScore(n)` sets the score directly, as a precondition. The extra-life and
  game-over rules still resolve through real play, so drive a real event to trigger
  them.
- `setLives(n)` sets the number of ships in reserve, as a precondition.
- `setInvuln(seconds)` sets the ship's post-respawn invulnerability window to
  `seconds` remaining; `0` clears it, so lethal collisions resume. This only sets the
  timer; whether a collision then kills the ship is decided by the real collision
  code.
- `setShip(state)` poses the ship. `state` may set any of `x`, `y`, `vx`, `vy`, and
  `angle` (facing, in radians). The ship keeps whatever fields `state` omits.
- `clearRocks()` removes every rock from the field, so a scenario can start from a
  known-empty field or isolate a single rock it adds.
- `addRock(size, state)` adds one rock and returns its index. `size` is `"large"`,
  `"medium"`, or `"small"`. `state` may set `x`, `y`, `vx`, and `vy`{{#if (eq variant.slug "warhead")}}, and
  `health` (the rock's remaining hits; defaults to full health for its size){{/if}}. The rock enters live
  play like any other, drifting and pulled by gravity when stepped.
- `addBullet(state)` places one of the ship's bullets in flight, as a precondition
  for a shot. `state` may set `x`, `y`, `vx`, and `vy`. The bullet then obeys gravity,
  wraps, and collides exactly like a fired one.
- `spawnSaucer()` brings a saucer onto the field now, as its normal cadence would,
  rather than waiting out the spawn timer. At most one saucer exists at a time.
- `setSaucer(state)` poses the current saucer. `state` may set `x`, `y`, `vx`, and
  `vy`. It has no effect if no saucer is present.
- `removeSaucer()` clears the current saucer from the field.
{{#if (eq variant.slug "warhead")}}
- `setTorpedoReady(ready)` sets the torpedo charge: `true` makes one torpedo charged
  and ready to fire now, `false` empties the charge and begins its recharge. This
  only arranges the charge; firing the torpedo (with the `F` key) still runs the real
  launch, homing, and impact code.
{{/if}}

A typical check calls `startGame()`, then `setShip`, `addRock`, and `addBullet` (or
`spawnSaucer` and `setSaucer`) to arrange the exact situation wanted, `step()` a
handful of ticks to run the real simulation, and reads the result from `snapshot()`.

### Input operations

The control operations above pose the world directly. The API can also inject
keyboard input, so a caller can drive the game the way a player does: navigate the
menus, start a game from the title, fly and shoot, pause, and toggle mute. Injected
input flows through the same handling the real keyboard feeds, exercising the actual
key bindings from `specs/rules.md` rather than a parallel path. Injecting input does
not take over the clock or the ship: a held movement key flies the ship through the
game's normal play code, so this is how a caller confirms the controls themselves
work.

- `keyDown(code)` presses a key down. `code` is a standard `KeyboardEvent.code`, for
  example `"ArrowUp"`, `"ArrowLeft"`, `"ArrowRight"`, `"KeyW"`, `"KeyA"`, `"KeyD"`,
  `"Space"`, `"Enter"`, `"Escape"`, `"KeyP"`, `"KeyM"`{{#if (eq variant.slug "warhead")}}, `"KeyF"`{{/if}}. The key becomes
  held, so a movement or thrust key drives the ship while it is held and the
  simulation is stepped in ticks, and any one-shot action the key triggers on the current
  screen (a menu move, a confirm, a pause, a mute toggle) is applied immediately.
- `keyUp(code)` releases a previously pressed key, ending its held state.
- `press(code)` is a convenience tap, a `keyDown` immediately followed by `keyUp`.
  This is the usual way to trigger a one-shot action (moving a menu selection,
  confirming it, firing a single shot, pausing, muting) without leaving the key held.

## Snapshot shape

`snapshot()` returns an object with at least these fields:

```js
{
  version: 1,
  screen: "title" | "howto" | "playing" | "paused" | "gameover",
  menuIndex: <number>,   // the highlighted entry (0-based) on a menu screen
  score: <number>,
  lives: <number>,       // ships in reserve
  wave: <number>,        // the current wave number (0 before the first spawns)
  waveBanner: <boolean>, // whether the WAVE N banner is currently showing
  muted: <boolean>,      // whether the mute toggle is currently on
  invuln: <number>,      // seconds of respawn invulnerability remaining (0 = none)
  ship: {
    x: <number>, y: <number>, vx: <number>, vy: <number>,
    angle: <number>,     // facing, in radians
    speed: <number>,     // the magnitude of the ship's velocity
    thrusting: <boolean>,// whether thrust is being applied this step
  },
  bullets: [
    { x: <number>, y: <number>, vx: <number>, vy: <number>, life: <number> },
  ],
  rocks: [
    { x: <number>, y: <number>, vx: <number>, vy: <number>,
      size: "large" | "medium" | "small", radius: <number>{{#if (eq variant.slug "warhead")}},
      health: <number> {{/if}}},
  ],
  saucer: { x: <number>, y: <number>, vx: <number>, vy: <number> } | null,
  enemyBullets: [
    { x: <number>, y: <number>, vx: <number>, vy: <number>, life: <number> },
  ],
{{#if (eq variant.slug "warhead")}}
  torpedoes: [
    { x: <number>, y: <number>, vx: <number>, vy: <number>,
      heading: <number>, life: <number> },  // heading in radians
  ],
  torpedoReady: <boolean>,   // whether a torpedo is charged and ready to fire
  torpedoRecharge: <number>, // fraction charged, 0 to 1 (1 = ready)
{{/if}}
  simTime: <number>,   // accumulated simulation time, in seconds
}
```

`speed` is the ship's current speed, the magnitude of its velocity. `invuln` is
positive while the ship is in its post-respawn grace window and `0` otherwise.
`life` on a bullet is the seconds it has left before it expires. `muted` reflects the
mute toggle, so a caller that presses the mute key can confirm it took effect.
{{#if (eq variant.slug "warhead")}}Each torpedo's `heading` is the direction it is currently traveling, in radians;
`torpedoRecharge` climbs from `0` to `1` as the single charge refills, and
`torpedoReady` is `true` once it is full.{{/if}}

## The debug overlay

Provide a read-only on-screen overlay showing the game's live internal state, so you
can watch what the simulation is doing while you play. It is toggled with the
backtick key (`` ` ``), off by default, and never changes gameplay; it only draws.

When on, it draws over the running game, legibly, in the game's monospace type, at
least: the current `screen`, the `score`, `lives`, and `wave`, the ship's position,
velocity, speed, and facing, the number of bullets and rocks in play, the saucer's
presence and position, and the remaining invulnerability, {{#if (eq variant.slug "warhead")}}the torpedo charge, {{/if}}the same facts
`snapshot()` reports. It is a diagnostic layer rather than part of the game's
presentation, so keep it visually plain and clearly separate from the HUD.
